A||||12||ToddAsh||Humming noise when turning right||||1||
Z||000000||ToddAsh||06-17-2001||09:58 AM|||| Hi, Can't figure this out. Got a humming noise when turning right even at very slow speeds.Removed,cleaned and checked wheel bearings,no play in front suspension,has new shocks. Car has only 16,000 miles on it.Oh,'85 V-6 SE. I'm sure someone will have the answer right away.This group is pretty amazing. Thanks,Todd Ash||||reg||1
Z||000001||SteveJ||06-17-2001||10:50 AM||||Did you make sure the splash shield on the inside of the brakes has enough clearance?||||reg||1
Z||000002||WKDFIRO||06-17-2001||04:21 PM||||You might just want to check the rear bearings as well. The sound they make can also be described as a hum but it can also resonate all through the car so you don't know where its coming from!||||reg||1
Z||000003||ToddAsh||06-17-2001||07:18 PM||||Well,the brake splash shield is not hitting and the noise is coming from the right front for sure.Kind of a low pitched hum.Never heard anything like it in 30 years of owning almost every kind of car. Todd||||reg||1
Z||000004||SteveJ||06-17-2001||07:26 PM||||Just for fun unplug the power line to both headlights.<P>How about the brakes not being lubed enough to slide and it rubbs the pad harder on the right turn because the caliper doesn't move away when touched by the rotor.||||reg||1
Z||000005||cadaver||06-18-2001||01:39 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by SteveJ:<BR><B>Just for fun unplug the power line to both headlights.<P>How about the brakes not being lubed enough to slide and it rubbs the pad harder on the right turn because the caliper doesn't move away when touched by the rotor.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><BR>That would mean the rotors are moving and I dont think that is happening as you would have a bunch of other problem.<P>Mine does the same thing. On mine its the radiator fan. The chassis flexs just enough for it to rub when cranked all the way over.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000006||terryk||06-18-2001||01:47 AM||||I vote for rear wheel bearing. They seem fine, but are brindled.<P>That's just how they sound.||||reg||1
Z||000007||SteveJ||06-18-2001||08:55 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by cadaver:<BR><B> [QUOTE]That would mean the rotors are moving and I dont think that is happening as you would have a bunch of other problem.<P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Of course the rotors move. They are connected to the hub which is taking all the lateral force of the turning car transmitted from the tire. Now it isn't much, maybe a couple thousandths, but if the caliper doesn't move back to accomidate it then it would rub. That's a within normal limits kind of thing.<P>I like the fan idea. <P>There isn't much else up front to make that kind of noise.||||reg||1
Z||000008||84Bill||06-18-2001||09:28 AM||||10 to 1 says it's a wheel bearing, front opposite direction of your turn eg noise when turning right, left bearing, ect.<p>[This message has been edited by 84Bill (edited 06-18-2001).]||||reg||14
Z||000009||cadaver||06-18-2001||10:00 PM||||Hey steve its not a "fan idea" it IS what mine does. ||||reg||1
Z||000010||ToddAsh||06-20-2001||10:22 PM||||Well I keep driving around in circles(right turn only)listening to this noise.The fan is off,bearings cleaned,greased and no play,brakes are 90%,I'm lost.Could this be in the rack? Dried out? Car is very low mileage but was started and driven once a week for 15 years.This sound is new,but I've recently started to use the car about 25 miles per week.Just remembered I took it to Carlisle,about 250 round trip and the noise started since then.It sounds like the hum of a pump or electric motor. My wifes Continental sounded like this when the air suspension would pump up periodicaly.Thanks Again, Todd Ash||||reg||1
